   "content": "Actually, never mind. Tell me about the venue instead.",
   "id": "01a0e110-3748-72c9-a63d-be2c790f0eb3"
  },
  {
   "role": "val",
   "content": "My lord, the reading will take place in House Armand’s Great Hall. The chamber is a vaulted stone room, the height of its ceilings echoing with the sound of spoken word. Across one long wall hang the house’s coat‑of‑arms—two wolves flanking a sword—tapestries that have weathered generations of counsel. A broad oak table, set with polished silver goblets and parchment scrolls, occupies the centre; benches of carved wood line the surrounding walls. Soft candlelight flickers from brass sconces, and a central hearth keeps the air warm even in winter. The hall’s acoustics have been refined over centuries to carry a single voice across the room, making it an ideal setting for a formal reading.",
   "id": "01a0e110-88e1-7ac9-9018-7d25650b50d2"
  }
 ],
